{"name":"Elephant Toothpaste","hex":"#c0e8d8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"elephant toothpaste","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of elephant's toothpaste."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/elephant_toothpaste"}],"entries":[{"title":"Elephant's toothpaste","blocks":[{"text":"Elephant's toothpaste is a hot foamy substance caused by the quick decomposition of hydrogen peroxide using potassium iodide (KI) or yeast and warm water as a catalyst. How rapidly the reaction proceeds primarily depends on the concentration of hydrogen peroxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/14._%D0%A1%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0_%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%B1%D0%B8.webm/330px--14._%D0%A1%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%BE%D0%B2%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0_%D0%BF%D0%B0%D1%81%D1%82%D0%B0_%D0%B7%D0%B0_%D0%B7%D0%B0%D0%B1%D0%B8.webm.jpg","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elephant's_toothpaste","wikidata":"Q5359401"},{"title":"Elephant","blocks":[{"text":"Elephants are the largest living land animals. Three living species are currently recognised: the African bush elephant, the African forest elephant, and the Asian elephant. They are the only surviving members of the family Elephantidae and the order Proboscidea; extinct relatives include mammoths and mastodons. Distinctive features of elephants include a long proboscis called a trunk, tusks, large ear flaps, pillar-like legs, and tough but sensitive grey skin. The trunk is prehensile, bringing food and water to the mouth and grasping objects. Tusks, which are derived from the incisor teeth, serve both as weapons and as tools for moving objects and digging. The large ear flaps assist in maintaining a constant body temperature as well as in communication. African elephants have larger ears and concave backs, whereas Asian elephants have smaller ears and convex or level backs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/African_Bush_Elephant.jpg/330px-African_Bush_Elephant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elephant","wikidata":"Q7378"},{"title":"Toothpaste","blocks":[{"text":"Toothpaste is a paste or gel dentifrice that is used with a toothbrush to clean and maintain the aesthetics of teeth. Toothpaste is used to promote oral hygiene: it is an abrasive that aids in removing dental plaque and food from the teeth, assists in suppressing halitosis, and delivers active ingredients to help prevent tooth decay and gum disease (gingivitis). Due to variations in composition and fluoride content, not all toothpastes are equally effective in maintaining oral health. The decline of tooth decay during the 20th century has been attributed to the introduction and regular use of fluoride-containing toothpastes worldwide. Large amounts of swallowed toothpaste can be poisonous. Common colors for toothpaste include white and blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Toothpasteonbrush.jpg/330px-Toothpasteonbrush.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toothpaste","wikidata":"Q35855"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Elephant_Toothpaste_Denver_JDS_Labs.jpg/500px-Elephant_Toothpaste_Denver_JDS_Labs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Elephant_Toothpaste_Denver_JDS_Labs.jpg/1280px-Elephant_Toothpaste_Denver_JDS_Labs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Elephant_Toothpaste_Denver_JDS_Labs.jpg/500px-Elephant_Toothpaste_Denver_JDS_Labs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Two people watching the reaction of Elephant's toothpaste","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AElephant_Toothpaste_Denver_JDS_Labs.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Materials%2C_2017%2C_elephant_toothpaste.jpg/500px-Materials%2C_2017%2C_elephant_toothpaste.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Materials%2C_2017%2C_elephant_toothpaste.jpg/1280px-Materials%2C_2017%2C_elephant_toothpaste.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/Materials%2C_2017%2C_elephant_toothpaste.jpg/500px-Materials%2C_2017%2C_elephant_toothpaste.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Materials for the experiment","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMaterials%2C_2017%2C_elephant_toothpaste.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Loxodonta_Elephas_distribution.png/500px-Loxodonta_Elephas_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Loxodonta_Elephas_distribution.png/500px-Loxodonta_Elephas_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALoxodonta_Elephas_distribution.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Deinotherium12.jpg/500px-Deinotherium12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Deinotherium12.jpg/1280px-Deinotherium12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Deinotherium12.jpg/500px-Deinotherium12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnai